nded  gaming session i don't think you could  hold  the beefy steam deck over your head  laying down for an extended period of  time i think either way you may feel  fatigued enough that you might drop it  on your face  but if you're just sitting down on a  couch and like casually playing in front  of you or you're commuting  um i think i think you'd have an okay  enough time  i should say an ad that the units we had  the demo with  aren't final they'd actually just gone  the units the day before we had arrived  so a lot of stuff still up in the air um  but speaking about the battery life if  you pl  unplug the unit from a power source the  uh overall  power of the unit isn't supposed to  change you're supposed to get the same  experience whether or not you're playing  hardwired plugged into power or you're  just running off the battery  i should add also that rory and i matt  rory from giantbomb also  we kept switching between a bunch of  games and we had the brightness jacked  all the way up as well as the audio so  that was probably affecting  the overall battery life for about maybe  like an hour and a half  running off of just battery constantly  switching between things  and not like doing a quick resume type  of thing just fully shutting down a game  and  waiting for something else to load up uh  the battery may have drained to maybe  30 35 rory didn't seem to have  that much fatigue he was playing a  straight more than i was  i guess one of the other things in terms  of ergonomics is  a lot of people have been looking at the  two sticks  at the very top of the device how was  that and also the curved button on the  side  how was it to hold and stretch your  thumbs up all the way and did you have  any issues where you were  because it's got the sticks it's got the  buttons and then it's got the touch pads  underneath um yeah any like crossover  there any issues with pr  playing it did you find the touch pads  to be useful  so unfortunately we didn't come across  any games that utilized the touchpads  that well  we tried disco elysium and you know  that's a point and click adventure type  of game so we were thinking  uh we'd be able to use the touch pads  but in that current build  um it wasn't optimized for that they  were i think  using how the control scheme that it is  on  playstation so uh it's relying more on  the analog sticks than touch pads  it felt very similar to holding a wii u  tablet  where the analog sticks are kind of  jacked all the way up it took some  getting used to  we fired up hades and hades you know  it's kind of burned into your  muscle memory if you've beaten the game  a couple times  and it took a little bit of time to  get acclimated to aiming and like  running around in it  but after a while like it i it didn't  feel too weird having the analog sticks  like  that far up on the unit um having your  hands rested in neutral they'd  be your thumbs would probably be on the  touch pads and even then  depending on whatever game you'd be  playing that could utilize them more  effectively i think it'd be okay  we we fired up doom eternal and rory was  having  uh a normal time playing through that  game uh we fired up  sea of thieves and you know just jump  literally jumping around  in the game i didn't feel any issues or  anything different and again it's just  like  getting your hands used to the uh  placement but the steam deck does have  the options to  turn off the paddles in the back it does  have paddles like a  an elite controller would i think you  can also turn off the touch pads uh  depending on the game by game basis  so there's just three models of the the  steam deck and  the main distinguishing features between  the cheaper model and the two the  mid-range and the high price range one  is the  um the lower end one has emmc uh  storage while the other one has nvme ssd  storage  do you know which model you're playing  and what kind what was it what was the  experience of like loading and switching  between games like for you  um i'm not 100 sure which model they had  us demo  but given our demo time with the unit  i think i would stray closer to at least  the mid-tier  option rather than the uh the  entry-level one with  like the 64 gig sd card i mean you can  slap in a different sd card of course  but the loading times i would assume uh  would be much better  not with uh the entry level unit  the baseline one um low times you know  for the most part  it it it was it was pretty good you know  we  we actually did a comparison run test uh  trying to load up hades on inside of  switch versus the steam deck  uh the steam deck had to run through its  initial os stuff  uh but then it wound up eclipsing it um  while the switch was still loading into  hades we ran the test again  except instead of trying to load into  hades we loaded into control  and then it like mid way while the  switch was still loading  into hades the steam deck had already  loaded into control and is ready at the  main menu  um yeah yeah i will say there  were a couple hitches in control i don't  know if that was because  you know there's still a pre-production  unit it isn't completely final  but we were actually because you know  it's it's  pretty much a pc in your hands we were  able to bump down  the um the video settings and we're  actually able to get a smoother 60  fps right right and and  the you mentioned that it is basically a  pc in your hands did you  get to do anything with the non-gaming  application  side of it like were you loading up like  fruity loops on there or anything like  what's going on  uh not fruity loops i wasn't able to get  my uh  producer mode uh up in seattle  but they did have a demo unit where they  had the steam deck  set up in like a desktop environment and  that was  it has a usbc port on top and they had  it going out to a usb  hub and then from the hub going an hdmi  out there  and a bluetooth mouse and keyboard and  this was the most surprising thing to me  because it was running  it had like a vlc playback going on loop  some coding instance going on uh it also  had blender going which is like a 3d  modeling software and it was also  running netflix  and factorio at the same time which was  super impressive  and there wasn't anything extra going on  it was just docked so you know you had  an ability with keyboard to do a little  bit more um  and that was super impressive because  you know a lot of people say that this  is like the nintendo switch killer but  upon having my actual own actual time  with it and just seeing how the devs are  approaching this machine  i don't think that way at all anymore  it's more so  getting people into the pc  sphere right getting them into the steam  ecosystem  and you know you mentioned fruity loops  they're super open about  how open the steam deck  can be they weren't shying around away  from like  yeah you know like you could probably  install xyz onto it  we're not gonna tell you how or if you  could do that but you  you have the ability to do so so you  know it feels super  like it will be super open where you  could install fruity loops  you could probably i don't know install  adobe premiere  and be editing with a controller you  know  which would be a nightmare but kind of  neat  well there's there's an idea for your  next video on a giant bomb or maybe  maybe we'll do it over here  or something thank you so much for  giving us your overall impressions um  obviously there's way more to the  experience of playing the  uh steam deck but i would encourage  everyone to head to giantbomb.com  there's a video on the site and also on  youtube where  jan and rory are hanging out and talking  about playing uh  their experience of it and it goes into  in-depth uh about  what they kind of feel about it which is  very very good keep an eye on  youtube.com four slash games but we'll  have plenty more  um discussion about the steam deck as we  get closer to the  release and also we'll probably throw in  some game gear chat as well because you  know  oh why not i've been ready let's go  i need to find the game here the tv  tuner  yeah jen thank you so much for your time  thank you tim

